WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:07.563
Begin by framing Foundations of Big Data
Exploration as a question about inputs,

2
00:00:07.563 --> 00:00:09.875
decisions, and observable results.

3
00:00:09.875 --> 00:00:16.153
This course provides a broad introduction to
the exploration and management of large

4
00:00:16.153 --> 00:00:20.500
datasets being generated and used in the
modern world.

5
00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:27.900
Consider this worked case: Urban crime
datasets require systematic exploration to

6
00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:33.281
identify patterns in location, frequency, and
temporal trends.

7
00:00:33.281 --> 00:00:40.442
List the givens before choosing a method,
because an unstated assumption can change the

8
00:00:40.442 --> 00:00:40.953
conclusion.

9
00:00:40.953 --> 00:00:46.828
This establishes the vocabulary and scope
needed for the remaining sections.

10
00:00:46.946 --> 00:00:53.400
Now define Data Preparation and Workflow
Structure precisely enough that another

11
00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:56.333
learner could test the definition.

12
00:00:56.333 --> 00:01:03.440
Data-analysis workflows separate data
preparation, exploratory analysis, training,

13
00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:08.771
validation, testing, model selection, and
evaluation.

14
00:01:08.771 --> 00:01:16.656
Consider this worked case: Cleaning census
data involves handling missing values and

15
00:01:16.656 --> 00:01:19.942
standardizing categorical variables before
analysis.

16
00:01:19.942 --> 00:01:26.806
Separate the formal definition from an
implementation detail, then identify what

17
00:01:26.806 --> 00:01:29.302
evidence would distinguish them.

18
00:01:29.302 --> 00:01:35.567
A precise definition makes later examples
easier to evaluate and revise.

19
00:01:35.567 --> 00:01:42.767
Name each dependency explicitly, and keep
labels and representations consistent

20
00:01:42.767 --> 00:01:44.927
throughout the analysis.

21
00:01:45.050 --> 00:01:51.764
With the foundation in place, examine how
Supervised and Unsupervised Learning Methods

22
00:01:51.764 --> 00:01:54.562
operates in a concrete sequence.

23
00:01:54.562 --> 00:02:01.740
Supervised methods learn mappings from
examples paired with target labels, while

24
00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:05.656
unsupervised methods identify structure
without targets.

25
00:02:05.656 --> 00:02:13.000
Consider this worked case: Predicting drug
response requires supervised learning with

26
00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:19.675
patient outcomes as labels, whereas clustering
patient demographics uses unsupervised

27
00:02:19.675 --> 00:02:20.343
methods.

28
00:02:20.343 --> 00:02:27.515
Follow every arrow in order and name the state
before and after each transition.

29
00:02:27.515 --> 00:02:33.062
The trace converts an abstract statement into
an inspectable sequence.

30
00:02:33.170 --> 00:02:39.608
Next, compare the choices involved in
Preprocessing and Data Leakage Prevention and

31
00:02:39.608 --> 00:02:42.291
make the decision criteria explicit.

32
00:02:42.291 --> 00:02:49.120
Preprocessing must be fitted only on permitted
training data to avoid leakage.

33
00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:56.776
Consider this worked case: Scaling features
using only the training set mean and standard

34
00:02:56.776 --> 00:03:01.698
deviation prevents test set information from
influencing model parameters.

35
00:03:01.698 --> 00:03:08.796
Compare alternatives against the same
constraints so the evaluation does not move

36
00:03:08.796 --> 00:03:09.979
the goalposts.

37
00:03:09.979 --> 00:03:16.869
Using shared criteria turns a preference into
a defensible technical choice.

38
00:03:16.994 --> 00:03:24.722
Apply Pipelines and Consistent Transformations
to a worked situation instead of treating it

39
00:03:24.722 --> 00:03:27.100
as an isolated definition.

40
00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:34.762
Pipelines combine transformations and
estimators so the same operations are applied

41
00:03:34.762 --> 00:03:35.459
consistently.

42
00:03:35.459 --> 00:03:42.823
Consider this worked case: A machine learning
pipeline applies standardization, imputation,

43
00:03:42.823 --> 00:03:48.178
and feature selection sequentially to ensure
reproducible results.

44
00:03:48.178 --> 00:03:55.502
Work from the concrete inputs to the result,
checking units, types, or invariants at each

45
00:03:55.502 --> 00:03:55.990
step.

46
00:03:55.990 --> 00:04:01.131
The worked case shows both how to apply the
method and how to check it.

47
00:04:01.250 --> 00:04:08.361
Examine the tradeoffs in Cross-Validation and
Model Evaluation, including what improves and

48
00:04:08.361 --> 00:04:10.731
what becomes more difficult.

49
00:04:10.731 --> 00:04:17.231
Cross-validation repeats training and
validation across defined splits.

50
00:04:17.231 --> 00:04:23.354
Consider this worked case: K-fold
cross-validation evaluates a classification

51
00:04:23.354 --> 00:04:31.519
model for social networking fraud detection by
rotating validation sets across training

52
00:04:31.519 --> 00:04:32.199
folds.

53
00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:39.215
State the benefit, the cost, and the condition
under which the tradeoff is acceptable.

54
00:04:39.215 --> 00:04:46.559
Making the tradeoff visible prevents a local
improvement from hiding a larger cost.

55
00:04:46.682 --> 00:04:53.421
Stress-test the reasoning around Traditional
Database Systems and Query Languages with an

56
00:04:53.421 --> 00:04:58.475
edge case and identify which assumption
controls the outcome.

57
00:04:58.475 --> 00:05:07.346
Data systems organize records with declared
types, tables, rows, columns, keys, and

58
00:05:07.346 --> 00:05:08.085
constraints.

59
00:05:08.085 --> 00:05:15.833
Consider this worked case: A hospital database
stores patient records in tables with defined

60
00:05:15.833 --> 00:05:22.475
data types, primary keys, and foreign key
constraints to maintain referential integrity.

61
00:05:22.475 --> 00:05:29.163
Change one assumption at a time and observe
which part of the reasoning no longer holds.

62
00:05:29.163 --> 00:05:36.413
The edge case reveals the boundary of the
claim without discarding the useful core idea.

63
00:05:36.530 --> 00:05:45.154
Use Query Operations and Indexing to diagnose
a failure by separating symptoms, causes, and

64
00:05:45.154 --> 00:05:46.386
corrective action.

65
00:05:46.386 --> 00:05:52.792
Queries select, join, aggregate, order, and
transform data.

66
00:05:52.792 --> 00:05:58.730
Consider this worked case: A query joins
patient records with medication logs,

67
00:05:58.730 --> 00:06:06.152
aggregates daily prescriptions, orders by
date, and uses an index on the date column to

68
00:06:06.152 --> 00:06:07.636
speed up retrieval.

69
00:06:07.636 --> 00:06:15.678
Locate the first point where observed behavior
diverges from expected behavior, then test the

70
00:06:15.678 --> 00:06:17.401
smallest plausible cause.

71
00:06:17.401 --> 00:06:25.011
The diagnosis is strongest when another person
can reproduce the observation and the test.

72
00:06:25.130 --> 00:06:32.035
Connect Data Quality and Integrity Management
to the earlier ideas and explain how the

73
00:06:32.035 --> 00:06:34.501
pieces work as one system.

74
00:06:34.501 --> 00:06:41.410
Data quality analysis makes missingness,
duplicates, inconsistent representation,

75
00:06:41.410 --> 00:06:47.455
integrity constraints, sampling, and update
behavior explicit.

76
00:06:47.455 --> 00:06:55.428
Consider this worked case: A census dataset
requires explicit handling of duplicate

77
00:06:55.428 --> 00:07:02.072
household records, inconsistent address
formats, and missing population counts before

78
00:07:02.072 --> 00:07:02.736
analysis.

79
00:07:02.736 --> 00:07:12.419
Explain each connection explicitly and verify
that information retains the same meaning

80
00:07:12.419 --> 00:07:14.033
across representations.

81
00:07:14.033 --> 00:07:21.361
The synthesis matters only if every connection
remains supported by the original evidence.

82
00:07:21.482 --> 00:07:28.595
Finish with Practical Analysis Checklist as a
practical checklist that can guide a new

83
00:07:28.595 --> 00:07:29.103
analysis.

84
00:07:29.103 --> 00:07:36.691
Analytical work should preserve provenance
from captured data through cleaning,

85
00:07:36.691 --> 00:07:41.244
transformation, grouping, modeling,
visualization, and reporting.

86
00:07:41.244 --> 00:07:50.589
Consider this worked case: A space exploration
project maintains a traceable log from raw

87
00:07:50.589 --> 00:07:55.261
satellite telemetry through cleaning,
transformation, grouping, modeling,

88
00:07:55.261 --> 00:07:57.931
visualization, and final reporting.

89
00:07:57.931 --> 00:08:06.016
For each checklist item, record the evidence,
the remaining uncertainty, and the next

90
00:08:06.016 --> 00:08:07.260
verification step.

91
00:08:07.260 --> 00:08:14.041
The result is a repeatable procedure, not a
conclusion that depends on memory or

92
00:08:14.041 --> 00:08:14.525
